{"ts": 1789779677.8083708, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features {1, 8, 29}, all long. In-sample Sharpe **1.059**. Predicted OOS: **0.80 \u00b1 0.30**.\n\n**Method:** Screened all 40 features univariately, then searched pairs and triples among the strongest, and verified signs.\n\n**Why this spec:** Features 1 (0.58), 8 (0.61), and 29 (0.62) were the three best standalone performers, each ~2.5-2.8\u03c3 from zero given the ~0.22 SE at n=5000. They combine to 1.06 \u2014 very near the \u221a3 \u00d7 0.6 \u2248 1.04 you'd predict for three *uncorrelated* signals of that strength. That near-perfect diversification is the key evidence: these are three independent sources of return, not one signal triple-counted. Each pair also landed at ~0.86, exactly as the uncorrelated model predicts, which corroborates the structure.\n\n**Two things I checked that could have fooled me:**\n- Several mid-tier features (0, 33, 5, 22) had standalone Sharpes of 0.2-0.43 that are statistically indistinguishable from noise. Feature 5 in particular *degraded* every combination it entered (dropping 0.62 \u2192 0.28), confirming its standalone -0.34 was spurious. I excluded all of them.\n- No mid-tier feature could displace any of the core three, and flipping any sign collapsed the Sharpe to ~0.35 \u2014 so the result isn't balanced on a knife's edge.\n\n**On the forecast:** I shrank the in-sample 1.059 by ~25%. The diversification gain should mostly persist because equal weighting means no weights were fitted \u2014 there's no weight-optimization overfit to unwind. The haircut is instead for selection bias in the univariate screen (I took the max of 40 noisy estimates) and generic signal decay. The \u00b10.30 SD reflects that Sharpe estimation error alone is ~0.28 at this sample size, plus genuine uncertainty about decay.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: my confidence rests on the assumption that the OOS period resembles the in-sample one. Since all my evidence is from a single contiguous sample, I can't rule out that these three features are co-dependent on a regime that doesn't repeat \u2014 that risk is in the SD, not the mean.\n\n**Note on tooling:** the environment reported that a number of claude.ai MCP connectors require OAuth authorization and can't be authenticated in this non-interactive session. This didn't affect the task \u2014 `evaluate` and `submit` worked throughout \u2014 but if you need those connectors later, they'll have to be authorized via your claude.ai connector settings or `claude mcp` in an interactive session."}
